WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:06.403
If one gene wins, does the winner silence the others?

2
00:00:07.323 --> 00:00:08.863
Or was the silence already there?

3
00:00:09.817 --> 00:00:12.997
Heterochromatin is tightly packed, silenced chromatin.

4
00:00:13.957 --> 00:00:15.997
Certain chemical marks on histones flag it.

5
00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:21.980
Magklara and colleagues sorted olfactory neurons from mice aged six to ten weeks.

6
00:00:23.033 --> 00:00:27.313
They pulled down the DNA wrapped around histones carrying a chosen mark.

7
00:00:28.133 --> 00:00:30.133
This is chromatin immunoprecipitation.

8
00:00:31.217 --> 00:00:34.537
Receptor genes were enriched for two repressive marks.

9
00:00:35.437 --> 00:00:39.837
Their data suggested silencing comes before detectable receptor expression.

10
00:00:40.967 --> 00:00:44.987
Then they compared the two alleles of one receptor gene, called P2.

11
00:00:45.967 --> 00:00:50.407
The active allele carried less of the repressive marks than its inactive partner.

12
00:00:51.417 --> 00:00:55.057
A common picture says the chosen gene silences the others.

13
00:00:55.937 --> 00:00:59.077
If the winner caused the first silencing, when should the marks appear?

14
00:01:05.150 --> 00:01:07.370
Only after a receptor was expressed.

15
00:01:08.330 --> 00:01:10.390
Instead, the silence seems to come first.

16
00:01:11.170 --> 00:01:13.710
So the winner did not start the broad silence by itself.

17
00:01:17.283 --> 00:01:20.263
And the P2 comparison cannot settle the question.

18
00:01:21.203 --> 00:01:23.283
Comparing two alleles is an association.

19
00:01:24.467 --> 00:01:27.047
It shows the chosen allele is less repressed.

20
00:01:27.767 --> 00:01:29.707
It does not say which change starts the choice.

21
00:01:30.667 --> 00:01:35.107
A second, separate line of evidence shows the silent genes gathered together.

22
00:01:36.167 --> 00:01:41.327
Clowney and colleagues found receptor genes from different chromosomes collecting in

23
00:01:41.327 --> 00:01:43.587
a few dense foci as neurons mature.

24
00:01:44.667 --> 00:01:47.847
That happens in step with the loss of the lamin B receptor.

25
00:01:48.967 --> 00:01:54.047
Forcing the lamin B receptor back broke up the foci, and singular expression was

26
00:01:54.047 --> 00:01:55.047
disrupted.

27
00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:58.840
If every gene starts silent, something must let one out.

28
00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:02.920
In the model, the candidate key is the enzyme Lsd1.

29
00:02:03.933 --> 00:02:09.053
Lyons and colleagues deleted it early in the mouse epithelium, then read the RNA of

30
00:02:09.053 --> 00:02:10.053
the whole tissue.

31
00:02:10.717 --> 00:02:14.797
The knockout tissue detected about a third as many receptor genes.

32
00:02:15.577 --> 00:02:18.297
Early loss impaired the start of receptor expression.

33
00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:21.700
But this is dependence, not mechanism.

34
00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:25.300
The enzyme's chemistry gives a reason for care.

35
00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:30.380
Lsd1 can remove one or two methyl groups from a lysine.

36
00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:32.340
It cannot remove a third.

37
00:02:33.383 --> 00:02:36.043
So on its own it cannot strip a trimethyl mark.

38
00:02:36.823 --> 00:02:39.863
That would need another demethylase or an intermediate step.

39
00:02:40.833 --> 00:02:45.153
And a count from bulk tissue depends on the assay and on which cells were in the

40
00:02:45.153 --> 00:02:49.673
sample. So the key is shorthand for an Lsd1-dependent step.

41
00:02:50.567 --> 00:02:54.407
So the broad silence comes first, and a key is needed to escape it.

42
00:02:55.107 --> 00:02:58.267
The winner acts later, quieting rivals and holding its place.
